    "result_text": "The generated mapper is now correct \u2014 `LocalityResolver.resolve(...)` is applied only to `locality`, and all other String fields map straight through.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `locality` field, derived from an owner's city via the fixed city-to-region table (Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD; otherwise `UNKNOWN`).\n\nChanges:\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `locality` string property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it in the `GET /api/owners/{id}` response alongside the other derived fields.\n2. **`src/main/java/.../util/LocalityResolver.java`** (new) \u2014 a small helper holding the fixed city-to-region table with a static `resolve(city)` that returns the canonical region or `\"UNKNOWN\"` (null-safe).\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a MapStruct `@Mapping` that populates `locality` via `LocalityResolver.resolve(owner.getCity())`.\n\n### One subtlety worth flagging\nMy first attempt put the lookup as a `default String locality(String)` method on the mapper interface. MapStruct treated that as a generic `String\u2192String` converter and silently applied it to **every** String property (name, address, telephone, \u2026), which broke owner creation (telephone `\"UNKNOWN\"` failed E.164 normalization). Moving the logic to a standalone helper referenced only through the expression fixed it \u2014 the regenerated impl confirms the resolver now touches only `locality`.\n\nAll tests pass (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, BUILD SUCCESS).",
